# tools/checker_paint_witness.rish -- a checkerboard SEEN on a real image proves itself on metal (Season G).
#
# Season G (Open Media Primitives) -- the discrete 2D sibling of tools/palette_paint_witness.rish: where the palette
# tiles ONE axis with a row of solid swatches, this rung tiles BOTH axes with an alternating two-color grid, the
# checkerboard a design surface draws as the TRANSPARENCY GROUND behind a translucent picture (the light-and-white
# squares an image editor shows so an alpha hole reads as a hole). Stands on the open geometry floor (image/shape.rye
# fill_rect), the open color algebra (image/color.rye), and the open QOI codec (image/qoi.rye).
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260816/20260816-210735_season-g-color-algebra-crux.md (the color floor this stands on).
# Teacher, thanked clean-room: gratitude/w3c-css-color.md (the W3C CSS Color spec + sRGB -- public standards only).
#
# The crux this witness proves is that a checkerboard becomes a real image: paint_checkerboard tiles the box with
# cell-sized squares, cell (col, row) painted its color by (col + row) parity. Each cell is its color exactly, the cells
# are contiguous and disjoint (no gap, no overlap), a boundary cell the cell size does not divide is clipped to the edge
# and still tiles exactly, a translucent color blends src-over, the parity is a true checker (orthogonal neighbors
# differ, diagonal neighbors share), the board round-trips through the open QOI codec byte-for-byte, paint_alpha_ground
# fills the conventional transparency ground, and a zero dimension, a zero cell size, and an out-of-bounds box refuse by
# name having painted nothing. Compose not braid -- shape owns the fill, qoi owns the target, color composites; this rung
# only routes each cell to its color. No network, no key, no funds.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/checker_paint_witness.rish

say "checker-paint: Language EN -- Season G's checkerboard painted onto an image; a keeper names two colors and a cell size and reads back a tiled two-color grid a surface can show as a transparency ground, on our own color model and open codec."
say "checker-paint: Style Radiant -- compose not braid; shape.fill_rect OWNS the fill, qoi owns the RGBA target, color composites src-over, and this rung only ROUTES each cell to its color by parity so a translucent cell tints the picture beneath."
say "checker-paint: Lens TAME -- the whole rectangle bound-checked ONCE up front in u64 so a far edge can never wrap; the cell count a ceiling division so a boundary cell is clipped not dropped; the cell partition in u32 so it never wraps; integer boundaries so a board is deterministic; no network, no key, no funds."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "image/bin/checker_paint"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p image/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build image/checker_paint.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "checker-paint: build failed -- ${build.err}"

let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "checker-paint: selftest exited non-zero -- ${selftest.out}"

let out = trim selftest.out
say "checker-paint: ${out}"

assert selftest.out contains "GREEN image-checker-paint" else "checker-paint: selftest did not print the GREEN checker-paint line"

assert selftest.out contains "round-trips through the open QOI codec byte-for-byte" else "checker-paint: the painted checker did not prove its QOI round trip"

assert selftest.out contains "a true checker: orthogonal neighbors differ, diagonal neighbors share" else "checker-paint: the parity did not prove it is a true checkerboard"

assert selftest.out contains "clips the last band to one pixel and still tiles to the edge" else "checker-paint: the non-dividing partition did not prove it clips and tiles exactly"

say "checker-paint: GREEN -- a checkerboard painted onto a real image (a true two-color parity grid, boundary cells clipped, the conventional transparency ground), the cells tiling exactly, each its color, its blend true, its round trip byte-for-byte, proven on metal."
